WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.869
-Very exciting.
Season 47 of "SNL."

00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:05.105
Three new cast members.
Ten new writers.

00:00:05.105 --> 00:00:07.106
-A hundred.
-A hundred new writers?

00:00:07.106 --> 00:00:08.908
-A hundred new writers.

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:11.310
I think it's part of
Biden's infrastructure plan?

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.079
-Oh, that's right. I saw that.

00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:15.481
He's like, "We got to
get more writers in there."

00:00:15.481 --> 00:00:18.351
-And the nice thing, I think,
is that luckily

00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:20.619
we never win an Emmy
for writing --

00:00:20.619 --> 00:00:22.688
because the statues
would bankrupt the Academy

00:00:22.688 --> 00:00:24.824
if they ever had to give statues
to the whole...

00:00:24.824 --> 00:00:26.459
-If they ever gave out a 100.

00:00:26.459 --> 00:00:28.027
-It would end the Academy,
so that's nice.

00:00:28.027 --> 00:00:30.330
-This is --
I was there when you started.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.831
It's crazy to me now that --

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:34.767
I mean, I was only there
four years before you started,

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:37.003
but you were a kid
when you showed up.

00:00:37.003 --> 00:00:38.938
And this is your I.D.
you still use.

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:40.873 line:0%
-This is my current I.D., yeah,
that I have.

00:00:40.873 --> 00:00:45.211 line:0%
-Current I.D., and they --
Do they believe it's you?

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:46.746
-Yeah, 'cause it still scans.

00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:48.514
Like, the security guards --

00:00:48.514 --> 00:00:50.483
It scans,
and that's what comes up.

00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:52.251
And they look at me,
and they're just like,

00:00:52.251 --> 00:00:56.089
"Whatever. Just come in.
At this point, just come in."

00:00:56.089 --> 00:00:58.824
It's truly like
if you had posted a photo

00:00:58.824 --> 00:01:00.293
like you were kidnapped,

00:01:00.293 --> 00:01:02.996
and there was a wanted photo out
there, and then they were like,

00:01:02.996 --> 00:01:06.699
"Let's just use it for his I.D.
for his job for 20 years."

00:01:06.699 --> 00:01:09.301
-"Hey, we found him. We rescued
him from the kidnappers.

00:01:09.301 --> 00:01:10.637
Let's use it as his I.D."

00:01:10.637 --> 00:01:12.405
-"Let's let him
never quite forget."

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:14.474
-People used to always
ask me this question.

00:01:14.474 --> 00:01:16.442
Were there summer stories
you were bummed

00:01:16.442 --> 00:01:19.746
not to be able to write sketches
about or tell jokes about?

00:01:19.746 --> 00:01:22.549
-Yes, there were some.
There were stories.

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:25.685
There was the Cleveland Indians.

00:01:25.685 --> 00:01:27.520
Did you see the --
They changed their name?

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.622
I don't know if you -- Do you --
Do you follow news stuff?

00:01:29.622 --> 00:01:30.956
-Yeah, I do. [ Laughs ]

00:01:30.956 --> 00:01:33.025
-The Cleveland Indians changed,
and there was a whole --

00:01:33.025 --> 00:01:35.161
What I like is there was
a whole press conference

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:36.629
where the guy came out
and he was like,

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:38.498
you know, "We're not
the Cleveland Indians anymore.

00:01:38.498 --> 00:01:41.334
We're gonna be the Cleveland
Guardians instead."

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:44.904
And I thought it would have been
really great if he'd come out

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:47.574
and been like,
"We're going with Redskins."

00:01:47.574 --> 00:01:50.410
[ Laughter ]

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.645
"We did some research,
and we discovered

00:01:52.645 --> 00:01:55.614
it actually recently
became available.

00:01:55.614 --> 00:01:57.950
And we'd like --
We're gonna do Redskins."

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:01.120
And I also really liked --
there was a story about --

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.223
that they caught
a Catholic priest on Grindr.

00:02:04.223 --> 00:02:05.991
Which I know you're like,
"And...?"

00:02:05.991 --> 00:02:11.463
But there was a whole outrage
about this was happening,

00:02:11.463 --> 00:02:15.601
and my thought was, "Isn't that
the best-case scenario?"

00:02:15.601 --> 00:02:18.438
Like, truly, isn't that
what we've all kind of

00:02:18.438 --> 00:02:20.840
wanted for years to happen?
For centuries.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.042
There's then this issue
where repressed gay men

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:25.211
didn't know who
they could open up to,

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:27.379
and now there's an app
on their phone

00:02:27.379 --> 00:02:29.715
that connects them to every
single gay man around them.

00:02:29.715 --> 00:02:31.451
-Right.
-Like Professor X

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:33.453
putting on a helmet
and being able to see

00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:35.254
all the mutants in the world.
-It's a Cerebro, right.

00:02:35.254 --> 00:02:37.490
-I don't know that he was
having sex with all of them.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.158
But, you know --
-He definitely wasn't.

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:40.759
-Could be why
he's in the wheelchair.

00:02:40.759 --> 00:02:42.228
We don't know, but...

00:02:42.228 --> 00:02:44.330
[ Laughter ]

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.332
I think that --
-He has Peyronie's?

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:47.933
[ Laughter ]

00:02:47.933 --> 00:02:50.569
Professor X is for --
-He went...

00:02:50.569 --> 00:02:53.405
Anyway, my point is,
I feel like kids out there

00:02:53.405 --> 00:02:56.142
should probably be pooling
their lunch money together

00:02:56.142 --> 00:02:58.845
to try to buy priests
premium access to Grindr.

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:00.413
-All right. That's really --

00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:03.549
-And that's my other commercial
they're gonna...

00:03:03.549 --> 00:03:05.185
-Hey, you were --

00:03:05.185 --> 00:03:07.720
-You do edits and stuff, right?
-No, no.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.323
Whenever we have people from
live TV shows, we do no edits.

00:03:10.323 --> 00:03:12.458
-Oh, wow. That's great.

00:03:12.458 --> 00:03:14.927
-You were on "Jeopardy!"

00:03:14.927 --> 00:03:16.663
You weren't on "Jeopardy!"
-No, I was not.

00:03:16.663 --> 00:03:18.598
-But your --
Let's just take a look.

00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:21.033
-That would almost make
more sense, by the way,

00:03:21.033 --> 00:03:22.702
if was the contestant,
than this.

00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:24.303
-Let's take a look.
-Yeah.

00:03:24.303 --> 00:03:26.105
-Recent Books for $800.

00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:33.579
Kelly?
-Who's Conan O'Brien?

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:35.048
-That's incorrect.

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:39.051
-[ Beeping ]
-Looking for Colin Jost.

00:03:39.051 --> 00:03:41.587
-All right. I think
that's a real burn on Conan.

00:03:41.587 --> 00:03:44.057
'Cause someone was like,
"I want to punch him."

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:45.958
-He was locked and loaded
with it,

00:03:45.958 --> 00:03:48.428
like, "Punchable face?
Conan. Go!"

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:51.630
-Now, is that something where
somebody tells you about it?

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:53.032
Like, how do you find out
it happened?

00:03:53.032 --> 00:03:57.604
-Ye, I found out it happened
because my mom sent me the clip,

00:03:57.604 --> 00:03:59.839
and she said,
"I would have gotten it."

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:02.608
[ Laughter ]

00:04:02.608 --> 00:04:04.210
Thanks.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:07.213
-She's like,
"I don't get Cosmo,

00:04:07.213 --> 00:04:08.714
but I would have gotten that."

00:04:08.714 --> 00:04:10.617
-And, yes, it's a very --

00:04:10.617 --> 00:04:12.885
'Cause when you make -- when
you're a clue on "Jeopardy!",

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:14.387
you feel like you've made it.

00:04:14.387 --> 00:04:16.355
-Yes.
-But then in that same moment,

00:04:16.355 --> 00:04:18.958
you realize, "Oh, I have not."
-Well, I think it's interesting

00:04:18.958 --> 00:04:20.994
to note how --
I mean, really what you want

00:04:20.994 --> 00:04:24.163
is to be like
a $100 first-round...

00:04:24.163 --> 00:04:26.032
You were $800.
You know what I mean?

00:04:26.032 --> 00:04:28.200
There was some value
to getting that right.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.636
-Yeah, I guess you do
have to work your way up --

00:04:30.636 --> 00:04:32.037
-To the gimme.

00:04:32.037 --> 00:04:33.939
You want to be in the Monday
"New York Times" crossword.

00:04:33.939 --> 00:04:35.841
-Yeah.
-You know what I mean?

00:04:35.841 --> 00:04:38.044
-Yeah, that's true.
I'm barely Saturday right now.

00:04:38.044 --> 00:04:40.713
-But let's -- Thank God
it didn't happen again.

00:04:40.713 --> 00:04:42.915
-Yeah. No. That would be --

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:45.018
-Let's try Harvard for $1,000.

00:04:45.018 --> 00:04:46.585
-Alex?

00:04:46.585 --> 00:04:49.588
-Writers and performers who have
gone from "The Lampoon"

00:04:49.588 --> 00:04:53.292
to "Saturday Night Live"
include this young fellow,

00:04:53.292 --> 00:04:56.696
who co-hosts "Weekend Update"
with Michael Che.

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:00.967
-[ Beeping ]
-And his name is Colin Jost.

00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:03.569
[ Laughter ]

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:05.738
-First of all,
they somehow found a photo

00:05:05.738 --> 00:05:07.740
worse than the one on my I.D.
-Yeah.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.375
-Then, the other thing is,

00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:13.012
Alex Trebeck went to Harvard
to a location,

00:05:13.012 --> 00:05:16.115
filmed a clue, like,
"You're gonna like this one."

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:17.717
-Yeah.
-Dead silence.

00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:19.184
-We're gonna use a picture.

00:05:19.184 --> 00:05:20.620
We're gonna talk about
who he works with.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.121
They really put that one on a...

00:05:22.121 --> 00:05:24.090
-Every context that you
could possibly give me.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:25.557
-Nothing.

00:05:25.557 --> 00:05:28.527
-And then you could tell
when he had to reveal the name.

00:05:28.527 --> 00:05:31.230
He just was like...
"Why did I do this?"

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:33.465
-That's really also
a double burn on Conan,

00:05:33.465 --> 00:05:34.801
who also went to Harvard.

00:05:34.801 --> 00:05:36.302
Nobody even guessed him
on that one.

00:05:36.302 --> 00:05:38.104
They only guessed him
on the punchable face.

00:05:38.104 --> 00:05:41.340
-We'd hit him, but I don't know
if he could go to Harvard.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:43.376
Yeah, so that's cool.

00:05:43.376 --> 00:05:46.178
-You won an award.
You won an Audie?

00:05:46.178 --> 00:05:48.180
-Yeah.
Were you going to say "outtie"?

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:50.650
-I think maybe --
-Did I just win an Audi?

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:52.551
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.551 --> 00:05:55.187
-I won an Audie, yes,
which is very exciting.

00:05:55.187 --> 00:06:00.059
I'm very conditioned
to not win any award ever,

00:06:00.059 --> 00:06:01.527
so it was very --

00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:03.696
I was very flattered
and very surprised.

00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:07.299
For Best Audiobook,
for my book, which was cool.

00:06:07.299 --> 00:06:08.767
-Which is
"Very Punchable Face."

00:06:08.767 --> 00:06:10.302
And so you recorded
your own book.

00:06:10.302 --> 00:06:11.637
-Yes.
-And you got an award.

00:06:11.637 --> 00:06:12.939
That is a genuinely cool thing.

00:06:12.939 --> 00:06:16.108
-It was so cool.
I didn't even know you could.

00:06:16.108 --> 00:06:17.710
It was very cool.

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:21.313
David Sedaris announced it, who
was a hero of mine as a writer.

00:06:21.313 --> 00:06:25.484
And -- When you go and you're
recording an audiobook,

00:06:25.484 --> 00:06:27.753
the other part of it
is you're hearing --

00:06:27.753 --> 00:06:29.188
You're reading out loud

00:06:29.188 --> 00:06:30.689
what you already wrote
and is locked in.

00:06:30.689 --> 00:06:32.525
And then as you're reading it
out loud, you're like,

00:06:32.525 --> 00:06:35.027
"Oh, I would have
made some changes.

00:06:35.027 --> 00:06:38.697
This whole paragraph
could have been a cut."

00:06:38.697 --> 00:06:41.167
And I'll be like, "Can I, like,
punch this up a little bit?"

00:06:41.167 --> 00:06:42.901
And the guy's like
"Sure. I don't care.

00:06:42.901 --> 00:06:44.503
You can do
whatever you want here."

00:06:44.503 --> 00:06:46.838
-"I thought you were just
reading it, man. Do whatever."

00:06:46.838 --> 00:06:49.041
-"I'm not even checking
that it's the same book."

00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:50.643
-Congrats on that.

00:06:50.643 --> 00:06:53.012
-I could go on to Stephen
King's "It" for a while.

00:06:53.012 --> 00:06:55.147
-Congrats on that. Congrats on
another season of "SNL."

00:06:55.147 --> 00:06:56.382
How many is this for you?

00:06:56.382 --> 00:06:58.017
I guess we could do the math.
16, 17?

00:06:58.017 --> 00:06:59.819
-Oh, God, dude. I don't know.

00:06:59.819 --> 00:07:02.320
-"SNL" returns for its
47th season this Saturday.

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:05.458
-Which sounds like a joke.
It really does. Season 47.

00:07:05.458 --> 00:07:07.426
-47th season
with 47 new writers.

00:07:07.426 --> 00:07:10.496
This Saturday at 11:30 p.m.
here on NBC with Owen Wilson

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:12.532
and musical guest
Kacey Musgraves.

